<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Viceroyalty of Peru</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1542">1542</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Viceroyalty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> was the administrative unit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> created in 1542 to govern most of Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>, made it a principal source of imperial revenue. Accounts of the Spanish Empire describe it, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, as one of the two primary administrative units of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru.jpg" alt="Viceroyalty of Peru" width="314" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MilenioscuroDerivative work:Janitoalevic · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Viceroyalty_of_Peru_(%2Bclaimed_Patagonian_and_Amazonic_territories)_(cropped).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> and his conquistadors arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in 1532, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-conquest-of-the-inca-empire">Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire</a> is traditionally dated from the encounter at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a> on 16 November 1532. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> was taken prisoner and executed on 26 July 1533. Pizarro founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> on 18 January 1535 as the City of the Kings. The conquerors then fell into conflict among themselves, and to end it King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles I of Spain</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> on 20 November 1542. They established the Viceroyalty, officially the Kingdom of Peru, in place of the governorships of New Castile and New Toledo, and moved the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> to Lima.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After almost forty years of administrative disorder, Viceroy Francisco de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> established between 1569 and 1581 the political and administrative framework that governed for many years. Between 1580 and 1650 large-scale mining took hold, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> refined by amalgamation using mercury from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huancavelica">Huancavelica</a>. Silver production peaked in 1610. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> period the viceroyalty lost territory: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> was created in 1717 and re-established permanently in 1740, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> in 1776. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> lost its place as the economic centre, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> remained the Crown's most valuable asset in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>.</p><p>Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-fernando-de-abascal">José Fernando de Abascal</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> the centre of the royalist counter-revolution, and all attempts at independence, such as the Cuzco Rebellion of 1814, were suppressed. The Capitulation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a>, signed on 9 December 1824, ended the royalist military effort, and the last strongholds, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/real-felipe-fortress">Real Felipe Fortress</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiloe">Chiloé Archipelago</a>, fell in 1826.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The rest of the viceroyalty depended administratively on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, whose University of San Marcos, founded in 1551, is the oldest institution of higher education on the American continent. The economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, mined by contract and free wage labourers and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">mita</a>, a system of unfree labour inherited from pre-Hispanic times. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> by the last quipucamayoc recorded 12 million inhabitants of Inca Peru, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, 45 years later, the figure was 1,100,000 Indians, a loss attributed largely to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and other Eurasian diseases. Inca cities were rebuilt as Spanish towns around a plaza, and the Church was prominent: in the 17th century, Lima, with 26,000 inhabitants, had nineteen churches and monasteries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Viceroyalty of Peru” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Martín Hurtado de Arbieto founded the Spanish town of San Francisco de la Victoria de Vilcabamba, the old capital was abandoned to the forest, and the region was absorbed into the <b>Viceroyalty of Peru</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-inca-state">Neo-Inca State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Upper Peru had been transferred from the <b>Viceroyalty of Peru</b> to the new Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata in 1776, so both Lima and Buenos Aires claimed it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…former viceroy Santiago de Liniers was crushed and he was shot, but Paraguay and Montevideo swore to the Regency and the <b>Viceroyalty of Peru</b> annexed Upper Peru, so the Junta sent armies against all three: the beginning of the Argentine War of Independence, before any independence was declared.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Viceroyalty of Peru</b> grew out of the conquest of the Inca Empire by Francisco Pizarro and his companions, who captured the Sapa Inca Atahualpa at Cajamarca in 1532 and entered Cuzco in 1533.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It was composed between 1620 and 1631 by Juan Pérez de Bocanegra, a Franciscan priest who was also chief examiner of native languages for the Archdiocese of Cuzco, and published in 1631 in the <b>Viceroyalty of Peru</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hanacpachap-cussicuinin">Hanacpachap cussicuinin</a></li><li><blockquote>Lima became the capital of the <b>Viceroyalty of Peru</b>, created in 1542, and the centre of Spanish rule in South America.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-18">18 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
